--- !u!114 &988095432
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 988095418}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2000dc9599fb1fb41841d29b2c91e2f1, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CSSSimulation.Networking.COC2StageDialogs
  speakerName: Instructor
  strippingAsset: {fileID: 0}
  strippingLines:
  - 'Stage 1 of 4: stripping. Before you touch anything, know what you''re holding.'
  - The UTP cable is four twisted pairs of copper - eight conductors in total - inside
    one outer jacket. UTP stands for Unshielded Twisted Pair. The twisting is what
    cancels out electrical interference, so never untwist more of it than you have
    to.
  - 'The crimping tool is really three tools in one: a cutter blade, a stripper notch,
    and the die that presses an RJ45 connector shut. For this stage you only need
    the stripper notch.'
  - Stripping means taking about one inch of the outer jacket off WITHOUT nicking
    the eight conductors underneath. A nicked conductor will still pass a continuity
    test today and break in the ceiling six months from now.
  - Drag the crimping tool onto the end of the cable to strip it.
  wiringAsset: {fileID: 0}
  wiringLines:
  - 'Stage 2 of 4: arranging the conductors.'
  - 'The RJ45 is the eight-position, eight-contact plug on the end of a network cable
    - eight pins, eight tiny gold blades. Hold it with the locking clip facing away
    from you and the pins toward you: pin 1 is on the left, pin 8 on the right.'
  - 'T568B, pin 1 through 8: white-orange, orange, white-green, blue, white-blue,
    green, white-brown, brown. T568A is the same list with the orange and green pairs
    swapped.'
  - A STRAIGHT-THROUGH cable uses the same standard on both ends - that's PC to switch,
    or PC to router. A CROSSOVER uses T568A on one end and T568B on the other - that's
    PC to PC, or switch to switch.
  - Untwist only as far as the connector, flatten the conductors into order, and
    drag each one into its pin. Both ends need all eight before you can crimp.
  crimpingAsset: {fileID: 0}
  crimpingLines:
  - 'Stage 3 of 4: crimping. This is the permanent part.'
  - Inside the crimping tool is a die - a shaped set of teeth. Squeezing the handles
    drives the RJ45's eight gold contacts down through the insulation of each conductor
    to bite the copper, and at the same moment presses a plastic wedge onto the outer
    jacket so the cable can't be pulled back out.
  - 'Check three things before you squeeze. One: the colours are still in order.
    Two: every conductor is pushed all the way to the front wall of the plug. Three:
    the outer jacket is inside the plug, not just the bare wires - that wedge has
    nothing to grip otherwise.'
  - One squeeze per end, and there is no undo. A bad crimp gets cut off and the end
    is rebuilt from scratch.
  - Drag the crimping tool onto each connector to crimp it.
  testingAsset: {fileID: 0}
  testingLines:
  - 'Stage 4 of 4: testing.'
  - This is an RJ45 LAN cable tester. It comes in two parts. The MAIN unit sends
    a signal out of pin 1, then pin 2, and so on up to pin 8. The REMOTE unit lights
    up whichever pin that signal actually arrived on.
  - The tester has no idea what colour any wire is. All it can tell you is 'something
    I sent out of pin 3 came back in at pin 6'. That map of which pin reaches which
    is called the wiremap, and it's the only thing that matters.
  - Both rows counting 1 to 8 in step is a good straight-through cable. A pin that
    never lights is an OPEN - that conductor doesn't reach the far end. Lights arriving
    out of order mean the pinout is wrong. Two lighting at once is a SHORT.
  - Drag the cable's connector into the tester's socket to run the test.
  passAsset: {fileID: 0}
  passLines:
  - Both rows lit 1 through 8 in step. Full continuity on all eight conductors, and
    the pinout matches the cable you were asked to build.
  - 'That''s a working cable, and that''s the complete procedure: strip, arrange,
    crimp, test.'
  failAsset: {fileID: 0}
  includeFailureReason: 1
  failLines:
  - The tester says this cable will not carry a link.
  failClosingLines:
  - A crimped RJ45 cannot be opened back up. In the field you would cut both ends
    off and rebuild the cable from the jacket up - so that's exactly what we're going
    to do.
  - Back to stripping. Take your time with the colour order this run.
  procedureReset: {fileID: 988095433}
  restartAutomaticallyOnFail: 1
  replayTutorialsOnRetry: 1
  OnTestPassed:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls:
      - m_Target: {fileID: 988095434}
        m_TargetAssemblyTypeName: CSSSimulation.Networking.COC2BothCablesGate, Assembly-CSharp
        m_MethodName: NotifyTestPassed
        m_Mode: 1
        m_Arguments:
          m_ObjectArgument: {fileID: 0}
          m_ObjectArgumentAssemblyTypeName: UnityEngine.Object, UnityEngine
          m_IntArgument: 0
          m_FloatArgument: 0
          m_StringArgument: 
          m_BoolArgument: 0
        m_CallState: 2
      - m_Target: {fileID: 988095436}
        m_TargetAssemblyTypeName: CSSSimulation.Networking.COC2AssessmentManager,
          Assembly-CSharp
        m_MethodName: NotifyTestPassed
        m_Mode: 1
        m_Arguments:
          m_ObjectArgument: {fileID: 0}
          m_ObjectArgumentAssemblyTypeName: UnityEngine.Object, UnityEngine
          m_IntArgument: 0
          m_FloatArgument: 0
          m_StringArgument: 
          m_BoolArgument: 0
        m_CallState: 2
  OnTestFailed:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls:
      - m_Target: {fileID: 988095436}
        m_TargetAssemblyTypeName: CSSSimulation.Networking.COC2AssessmentManager,
          Assembly-CSharp
        m_MethodName: NotifyTestFailed
        m_Mode: 1
        m_Arguments:
          m_ObjectArgument: {fileID: 0}
          m_ObjectArgumentAssemblyTypeName: UnityEngine.Object, UnityEngine
          m_IntArgument: 0
          m_FloatArgument: 0
          m_StringArgument: 
          m_BoolArgument: 0
        m_CallState: 2
  keepPhysicsInSyncWithTransforms: 1
